---

<WeekPost
  title="Workshop 3: Local Model APIs"
  description="Connect your browser to a model running on your own computer, then inspect what you send and what comes back."
  week={12}
  year={ACTIVE_YEAR}
  heroImage={localModels}
  heroImageAlt="A language model moves into the menu bar. Promotional screenshot from [Llama App](https://llama.app/), showing installed models and a localhost address. The models pictured are examples from the app’s website; we’ll use Qwen2.5 1.5B for this workshop."
  contentClass="llama-content"
  pageLinks={pageLinks}
>
  <section
    slot="sidebar"
    class="llama-reference"
    aria-labelledby="llama-reference-title"
  >
    <h2 id="llama-reference-title" class="sidebar-section-title">
      Quick reference
    </h2>
    <dl>
      <dt>Inference</dt>
      <dd>
        Running a trained model to get a reply. It doesn’t change the model.
      </dd>
      <dt><code>127.0.0.1</code></dt>
      <dd>An address that always points back to your own computer.</dd>
      <dt><code>GET /v1/models</code></dt>
      <dd>Asks the app which models it has.</dd>
      <dt><code>POST /v1/chat/completions</code></dt>
      <dd>Sends messages to a model and returns its reply.</dd>
      <dt><code>choices[0].message.content</code></dt>
      <dd>Where the reply text appears in the response.</dd>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <div class="llama-intro">
    <p>
      In this workshop, you’ll connect this page to a model running on your own
      computer. You’ll send it messages, look at what your browser sends and
      what comes back, and see how instructions, structured data, and tool calls
      change what the model does.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="why-local" class="llama-step">Remote models and local models</h2>
  <div class="llama-stack">
    <p>
      You’re probably used to remote models, like ChatGPT or Claude. A company
      runs the model on its own servers, and your messages travel over the
      internet to reach it. A local model changes that setup.
    </p>
    <p><strong>With a remote model:</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Your messages are sent to a company’s servers.</li>
      <li>
        You pay for access, with a subscription or an API cost for every
        request, and you usually need an account and an API key.
      </li>
      <li>You need an internet connection and may hit rate limits.</li>
      <li>The company decides which models exist and when they change.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><strong>With a local model:</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Your messages stay on your computer.</li>
      <li>
        There’s no API cost or key. Once the model is downloaded, you can send
        as many requests as you like.
      </li>
      <li>It works offline.</li>
      <li>
        You choose the model and its settings. The limit is your computer: a
        small local model is less capable than a large remote one, and its speed
        depends on your memory and processor.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      A model you can run freely is a good place to experiment, because you can
      try things, break things, and look closely at every request without
      worrying about cost. Two free apps can run one on your computer: Llama App
      and Ollama. Both download a model, run it, and let a web page talk to it
      through a local address. This workshop uses Qwen2.5 1.5B. Qwen (通义千问)
      is a family of open models from the Qwen Team at Alibaba Cloud.
    </p>
    <p>
      When you send a message, the model uses what it already learned to write a
      reply. This is called <strong>inference</strong>. In the Markov workshop,
      you trained a model by counting patterns in a text. Here, you don’t change
      the model at all. You change only what you give it, like the instructions
      and the messages, so the same model can respond very differently. <a
        href="https://llama.app/docs/api">Llama App’s local API documentation</a
      >
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="llama-connect" class="llama-step">1. Connect a local model</h2>
  <div class="llama-stack">
    <p>
      Set up one of these apps and download a text model. Qwen2.5 1.5B is a
      small starting point; you can select another installed model to compare
      its answers. You only need one app.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Llama App (macOS or Windows 11)</strong>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Download the app.</strong>
            <a href="https://llama.app/docs/installation"
              >Llama App installation</a
            >
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Download the model.</strong> Open Qwen’s GGUF page and choose
            <strong>Use this model → Llama</strong>. <a
              href="https://huggingface.co/Qwen/Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct-GGUF"
              >Qwen GGUF model</a
            >
          </li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Ollama (macOS, Windows, or Linux)</strong>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Download the app.</strong>
            <a href="https://ollama.com/download">Ollama installation</a>
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Download the model.</strong> Select <code>qwen2.5:1.5b</code
            > in the app, or run <code>ollama pull qwen2.5:1.5b</code> in a terminal.
            <a href="https://ollama.com/library/qwen2.5:1.5b">Qwen on Ollama</a>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>Then connect this page to your app:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Keep your app open.</li>
      <li>Choose your app in the panel below.</li>
      <li>Click <strong>Find models</strong>.</li>
      <li>
        Select the Qwen model. The Flappy Bird exercise in step 4 needs a model
        that supports tool calls.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="week-panel">
      <p class="week-panel-header">
        How to read <code>Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct</code>
      </p>
      <dl class="llama-panel-body llama-model-guide">
        <dt><code>qwen2.5</code></dt>
        <dd>The model family (Qwen) and its version (2.5).</dd>
        <dt><code>1.5b</code></dt>
        <dd>
          The model’s size: about 1.5 billion parameters. Parameters are the
          numbers a model adjusts while it learns. More parameters usually means
          a more capable model that needs more memory.
        </dd>
        <dt><code>Instruct</code></dt>
        <dd>
          The model was trained to follow instructions and hold a conversation,
          not only to continue a piece of text.
        </dd>
        <dt><code>Q4_K_M</code></dt>
        <dd>
          A compression setting you may see on download files. It stores the
          model’s numbers with less precision, so the file is smaller and uses
          less memory, usually with a small loss in quality.
        </dd>
      </dl>
    </div>
    <figure class="lecture-figure llama-source-figure">
      <Image
        src={qwenSizes}
        alt="Qwen Team’s Qwen2.5 comparison chart with a column for each model size."
        widths={[480, 860, 1440]}
        sizes="(max-width: 680px) 100vw, 860px"
        data-no-dither
      />
      <figcaption>
        Qwen2.5 comes in several sizes. Compare the 1.5B column with a larger
        one to see how the family changes with size. <a
          href="https://qwenlm.github.io/blog/qwen2.5/"
          >Qwen2.5 release announcement</a
        >
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure class="lecture-figure llama-setup">
      <Image
        src={llamaDesktop}
        alt="Llama App’s menu showing installed and recommended models."
        data-no-dither
      />
      <figcaption>
        In Llama App, click the download arrow next to a recommended model. <a
          href="https://llama.app/docs/quickstart">Screenshot from Llama App.</a
        >
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="week-panel">
      <p class="week-panel-header">Local connection</p>
      <div class="llama-panel-body">
        <label class="llama-field">
          App
          <select id="llama-runtime">
            <option value="llama">Llama App</option>
            <option value="ollama">Ollama</option>
          </select>
        </label>
        <p>
          Local address <code id="llama-runtime-address"
            >http://127.0.0.1:9931</code
          >
        </p>
        <div class="llama-toolbar">
          <button id="llama-find" type="button" class="button"
            >Find models</button
          >
          <p id="llama-connection-status" role="status">Waiting to connect.</p>
        </div>
        <label class="llama-field">
          Model
          <select id="llama-model" disabled>
            <option value="">Connect to load your models</option>
          </select>
        </label>
        <p id="llama-model-details" hidden></p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <details class="week-toc">
      <summary
        ><span class="toc-heading-link">Connection help</span><span
          class="toc-disclosure"
          aria-hidden="true"></span></summary
      >
      <p>
        Keep your app running. If your browser asks to allow local network
        access, choose Allow, because this page needs it to reach the app. If
        you use Ollama on the published class site, add the site’s exact address
        to Ollama’s <code>OLLAMA_ORIGINS</code> setting and restart Ollama. You can
        also avoid this by opening a local preview from <code>127.0.0.1</code>. <a
          href="https://docs.ollama.com/faq#how-can-i-allow-additional-web-origins-to-access-ollama"
          >Ollama browser access</a
        >
      </p>
    </details>
  </div>

  <h2 id="llama-prompt" class="llama-step">2. Write a system message</h2>
  <div class="llama-stack">
    <p>
      A <strong>system message</strong> is a set of instructions that comes before
      the conversation. It tells the model who to be and how to respond. Choose an
      example role or write your own, then change it to hear how the same model sounds
      different.
    </p>
    <section class="week-panel" id="llama-chat-settings">
      <p class="week-panel-header">Role and settings</p>
      <div class="llama-panel-body">
        <label class="llama-field">
          Role
          <select id="llama-persona">
            {
              personas.map((persona) => (
                <option data-persona={JSON.stringify(persona)}>
                  {persona.label}
                </option>
              ))
            }
          </select>
        </label>
        <label class="llama-field">
          System message
          <textarea
            id="llama-system"
            rows="4"
            maxlength="4000"
            set:text={personas[0].instructions}
          />
        </label>
      </div>
      <details class="week-toc llama-code">
        <summary
          ><span class="toc-heading-link"
            >Temperature, top-p, and output limit</span
          ><span class="toc-disclosure" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary
        >
        <div class="llama-panel-body llama-settings">
          <div>
            <label class="llama-field">
              <span
                >Temperature <span
                  class="llama-slider-value"
                  data-slider-value="llama-temperature"
                  aria-hidden="true">0.8</span
                ></span
              >
              <input
                id="llama-temperature"
                type="range"
                min="0"
                max="2"
                step="0.1"
                value="0.8"
                aria-describedby="llama-temperature-help"
              />
            </label>
            <p id="llama-temperature-help">
              Controls how predictable the reply is. At <code>0</code>, the
              model picks the most likely next token every time. Higher values
              let less likely tokens in, so replies get more varied and less
              reliable. Try <code>0</code> and <code>1</code> with the same message.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <label class="llama-field">
              <span
                >Top-p <span
                  class="llama-slider-value"
                  data-slider-value="llama-top-p"
                  aria-hidden="true">0.9</span
                ></span
              >
              <input
                id="llama-top-p"
                type="range"
                min="0.05"
                max="1"
                step="0.05"
                value="0.9"
                aria-describedby="llama-top-p-help"
              />
            </label>
            <p id="llama-top-p-help">
              Limits the model to the most likely tokens that together add up to
              this share of the probability. A smaller value means fewer choices
              and safer replies.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <label class="llama-field">
              <span
                >Reply token allowance <span
                  class="llama-slider-value"
                  data-slider-value="llama-limit"
                  aria-hidden="true">192</span
                ></span
              >
              <input
                id="llama-limit"
                type="range"
                min="16"
                max="2048"
                step="16"
                value="192"
                aria-describedby="llama-limit-help"
              />
            </label>
            <p id="llama-limit-help">
              A token is a word, part of a word, or a punctuation mark. Defaults
              adjust to the selected model. These exercises skip optional
              reasoning in Llama App for quicker replies. Raise the allowance if
              a reply stops in the middle of a sentence.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </details>
    </section>
    <details class="week-toc llama-code">
      <summary
        ><span class="toc-heading-link">Context window in Llama App</span><span
          class="toc-disclosure"
          aria-hidden="true"></span></summary
      >
      <figure class="lecture-figure llama-setup">
        <Image
          src={llamaContext}
          alt="Llama App menu with context window options for a model."
          data-no-dither
        />
        <figcaption>
          <strong>Context length</strong> is how much text the model can read at
          once, including your messages and its replies. <strong
            >Max output tokens</strong
          > is a separate limit on how long a single reply can be. A larger context
          uses more memory. <a href="https://llama.app/docs/quickstart"
            >Screenshot from the Llama App quickstart.</a
          >
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </details>
  </div>

  <h2 id="llama-request" class="llama-step">3. Chat with the model</h2>
  <div class="llama-stack">
    <p>
      Send a message and read the reply. Then try the same message in another
      language, or ask the model to answer in a different one. Qwen2.5 <a
        href="https://qwenlm.github.io/blog/qwen2.5/"
        >lists support for more than 29 languages</a
      >, but a small model can be uneven. Notice where the role stays
      consistent, where the grammar slips, and what changes when you switch
      languages in the middle of a conversation.
    </p>
    <section class="week-panel" aria-labelledby="llama-chat-persona">
      <div class="week-panel-header conversation-header">
        <div>
          <strong id="llama-chat-persona" set:text={personas[0].name} />
          <p id="llama-chat-scene" set:text={personas[0].opening} />
        </div>
        <button type="button" class="llama-example" id="llama-chat-reset"
          >New chat</button
        >
      </div>
      <div class="llama-panel-body">
        <ol
          class="conversation-log"
          id="llama-chat-log"
          aria-label="Chat transcript"
          aria-live="polite"
        >
        </ol>
        <form id="llama-chat-form" class="conversation-composer">
          <label class="llama-field">
            Your message
            <textarea
              id="llama-user"
              rows="2"
              maxlength="1200"
              placeholder="Write a message. Try another language too."
              required></textarea>
          </label>
          <div class="llama-toolbar">
            <button type="submit" class="button" id="llama-send" disabled
              >Send</button
            >
            <button type="button" class="llama-example" id="llama-cancel" hidden
              >Stop reply</button
            >
            <p id="llama-chat-status" role="status"></p>
          </div>
        </form>
        <details class="week-toc llama-code">
          <summary
            ><span class="toc-heading-link">Inspect the latest exchange</span
            ><span class="toc-disclosure" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary
          >
          <p>
            Open this to see exactly what your browser sent and what came back.
            Each request includes the system message and the last six exchanges.
          </p>
          <pre><code id="llama-request-json" /></pre>
          <pre><code id="llama-response-json" /></pre>
        </details>
      </div>
    </section>
    <MunusNote label="System messages and chat history">
      <p>
        The model doesn’t remember your earlier messages. Each time you send
        one, the page sends the <code>system</code> message and the recent conversation
        again, and the model reads all of it before replying. That’s why a follow-up
        question can refer back to something you said before.
      </p>
    </MunusNote>
  </div>

  <h2 id="llama-tools" class="llama-step">
    4. Tool calls and structured output
  </h2>
  <div class="llama-stack">
    <p>
      A model can do more than write text. Here, it chooses an action. In
      <strong>Arcade</strong> mode, press Space or tap to flap and fly. In
      <strong>Model Autopilot</strong> mode, press Start and watch the model fly.
      The page asks it for a new move again and again until the bird crashes or you
      stop it. See how many pipes it can clear.
    </p>
    <div class="week-panel" id="llama-bird">
      <div class="week-panel-header bird-header">
        <strong>Flappy Bird</strong>
        <div class="bird-modes" role="group" aria-label="Game mode">
          <button type="button" data-bird-mode="arcade" aria-pressed="true"
            >Arcade</button
          >
          <button type="button" data-bird-mode="llama" aria-pressed="false"
            >Model Autopilot</button
          >
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="llama-panel-body bird-body">
        <div class="bird-frame">
          <button
            id="bird-stage-control"
            type="button"
            aria-label="Flap in Arcade mode"
          >
            <canvas id="bird-stage" width="480" height="270" aria-hidden="true"
            ></canvas>
            <span id="bird-overlay" aria-hidden="true"
              ><strong id="bird-overlay-title">Ready to fly?</strong><span
                id="bird-overlay-hint">Space or tap to flap</span
              ></span
            >
          </button>
          <div class="bird-scoreboard" aria-label="Scores">
            <span>Pipes <strong id="bird-score">0</strong></span>
            <span>Best <strong id="bird-best">0</strong></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="llama-toolbar bird-controls">
          <button type="button" class="button" data-bird-move="flap"
            >Flap</button
          >
          <div id="bird-pilot-controls" hidden>
            <button type="button" class="button" id="bird-ask" disabled
              >Start autopilot</button
            >
          </div>
          <button type="button" class="llama-example" id="bird-pause" disabled
            >Pause</button
          >
          <button type="button" class="llama-example" id="bird-reset"
            >Reset game</button
          >
        </div>
        <p id="bird-status" role="status">
          Press Space on the game or tap the board to start.
        </p>
        <ol id="bird-flight-log" aria-label="Recent model moves" hidden></ol>
        <details class="week-toc llama-code bird-code" id="bird-inspect" hidden>
          <summary
            ><span class="toc-heading-link"
              >Inspect the latest observation, tool, and reply</span
            ><span class="toc-disclosure" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary
          >
          <pre><code id="bird-inspector" /></pre>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p>
      Each turn, the page tells the model where the bird is and what the game
      predicts will happen after <code>flap</code> or <code>glide</code>. The
      model replies by calling a tool named <code>move_bird</code>. The page
      checks the call, makes the move, and sends the next situation.
    </p>
    <MunusNote label="Tool calls: propose, validate, execute">
      <p>
        A <strong>tool call</strong> is how a model asks for something to happen.
        You describe a tool and the inputs it accepts, and the model can propose
        a call, like <code>move_bird</code> with the action
        <code>flap</code>. The model doesn’t move the bird. Your code checks the
        proposal, decides whether to run it, and sends back what happened.
      </p>
    </MunusNote>
  </div>

  <h3 id="llama-shared-drawing">Color an illustration together</h3>
  <div class="llama-stack">
    <p>
      Now the model works with you on a shared drawing. Pick a color and click
      an area to fill it. The model can color the whole illustration, filling
      any areas you haven’t colored yet. Leave art direction blank for natural,
      varied colors, or describe the colors and mood you want. Both you and the
      model can use any RGB color. You can repaint any area at any time.
    </p>
    <section
      class="week-panel"
      id="llama-canvas"
      aria-labelledby="canvas-title"
    >
      <div class="week-panel-header coloring-header">
        <strong id="canvas-title">Coloring illustration</strong>
        <span id="canvas-count"></span>
      </div>
      <div class="llama-panel-body">
        <canvas
          width="720"
          height="480"
          aria-label="Text-free coloring illustration. Click an area to fill it with the selected color."
        ></canvas>
        <label class="llama-field coloring-picker">
          Your color
          <input id="canvas-color" type="color" value="#f28ab5" />
        </label>
        <div class="coloring-controls">
          <label class="llama-field">
            Your art direction for the model (optional)
            <textarea
              id="canvas-brief"
              rows="3"
              maxlength="500"
              placeholder="Describe the colors or mood you want, like “a quiet winter morning.”"
            ></textarea>
          </label>
          <button type="button" class="button" id="canvas-toggle" disabled
            >Start model coloring</button
          >
        </div>
        <div class="llama-toolbar">
          <button type="button" class="llama-example" id="canvas-clear"
            >Reset illustration</button
          >
          <p id="canvas-status" role="status">
            Choose a color, then click the illustration.
          </p>
        </div>
        <details class="week-toc llama-code">
          <summary
            ><span class="toc-heading-link"
              >Inspect the latest color decision</span
            ><span class="toc-disclosure" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary
          >
          <pre><code id="canvas-json">The latest color decision will appear here.</code></pre>
        </details>
      </div>
    </section>
    <MunusNote label="Structured output and shared state">
      <p>
        Normally a model writes free text. <strong>Structured output</strong> makes
        it reply in a fixed format that your code can read. Here, a JSON schema requires
        a region name and a hex color code like <code>#4a90d9</code>, and the
        page checks both before it paints. Each request also includes your art
        direction and the colors used so far, so the model can respond to what
        you’ve done.
      </p>
    </MunusNote>
  </div>

  <h2 id="llama-agents" class="llama-step">5. Let two speakers talk</h2>
  <div class="llama-stack">
    <p>
      One model plays both speakers by taking turns. Each speaker has their own
      system message, and both share the same setting. Choose an example pair or
      write your own, then press Start. Change a speaker’s instructions and run
      it again to see how small edits change the conversation. Try the pirate
      and chef pair to see whether each speaker stays in their own language.
    </p>
    <section class="week-panel" id="llama-stage" aria-labelledby="stage-title">
      <p class="week-panel-header" id="stage-title">Two speakers</p>
      <div class="llama-panel-body">
        <label class="llama-field">
          Example pair
          <select id="stage-preset">
            {
              stagePresets.map((preset) => (
                <option data-preset={JSON.stringify(preset)}>
                  {preset.label}
                </option>
              ))
            }
          </select>
        </label>
        <div class="stage-cast">
          {
            stagePresets[0].speakers.map((speaker) => (
              <div data-actor>
                <label class="llama-field">
                  Speaker name
                  <input type="text" maxlength="30" value={speaker.name} />
                </label>
                <label class="llama-field">
                  System message
                  <textarea
                    maxlength="240"
                    rows="3"
                    placeholder="Write an instruction for this speaker."
                    set:text={speaker.instructions}
                  />
                </label>
              </div>
            ))
          }
        </div>
        <label class="llama-field">
          Setting
          <textarea
            id="stage-direction"
            rows="2"
            maxlength="300"
            placeholder="Describe where they are or what they’re discussing."
            set:text={stagePresets[0].context}
          />
        </label>
        <div class="llama-toolbar">
          <button type="button" class="button" id="stage-toggle" disabled
            >Start conversation</button
          >
          <button type="button" class="llama-example" id="stage-reset"
            >Reset conversation</button
          >
          <p id="stage-status" role="status"></p>
        </div>
        <ol
          class="conversation-log"
          id="stage-dialogue"
          aria-label="Conversation"
          aria-live="polite"
        >
        </ol>
        <details class="week-toc llama-code">
          <summary
            ><span class="toc-heading-link">Inspect the latest request</span
            ><span class="toc-disclosure" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary
          >
          <pre><code id="stage-json">The latest request and response will appear here.</code></pre>
        </details>
      </div>
    </section>
    <MunusNote label="Roles and conversation history">
      <p>
        Both speakers are the same model. The page switches between two system
        messages and sends the conversation so far with each request. For each
        speaker, their own earlier lines are labeled as the assistant and the
        other speaker’s lines as the user, which is how the model can answer
        someone else. Small models often drift out of character or repeat each
        other, so watch for that.
      </p>
    </MunusNote>
  </div>

  <h2 id="llama-mcp" class="llama-step">Going further: MCP</h2>
  <div class="llama-stack">
    <p>
      The Flappy Bird tool, <code>move_bird</code>, is defined inside this page.
      <strong>MCP (Model Context Protocol)</strong> is an open standard for connecting
      a model to tools in other apps. An app acting as the host can find those tools,
      show their names and inputs to your local model, and run the calls the model
      requests. The host decides which tools the model is allowed to use. <a
        href="https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/learn/architecture"
        >MCP architecture</a
      >
      <a href="https://llama.app/docs/agents">Connect Llama App to a host</a>
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>GitHub.</strong> Read an issue, summarize a pull request, or check
        which changes are waiting for review. <a
          href="https://github.com/github/github-mcp-server"
          >GitHub MCP server</a
        >
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Library documentation.</strong> Look up examples for the exact version
        of a library you’re using. <a href="https://github.com/upstash/context7"
          >Context7</a
        >
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Browser testing.</strong> Open your sketch, click its buttons, and
        check that they work. <a
          href="https://github.com/microsoft/playwright-mcp"
          >Playwright MCP server</a
        >
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <h2 id="llama-reuse" class="llama-step">Start your own JavaScript sketch</h2>
  <div class="llama-stack llama-starters">
    <p>
      Create a <code>sketch.js</code> file and load it in your page with
      <code>&lt;script type="module" src="./sketch.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;</code
      >. Serve the page locally, for example with VS Code Live Preview. First,
      paste in the shared setup code below. Then add one of the examples under
      it. Keep your app open while the sketch runs.
    </p>
    <CodeSnippet
      code={llamaSetup}
      language="js"
      title="Shared setup for sketch.js"
    />
    {
      llamaStarters.map(({ title, guide, code }) => (
        <details class="week-toc llama-starter">
          <summary>
            <>
              <span class="toc-heading-link">{title}</span>
              <span class="toc-disclosure" aria-hidden="true" />
            </>
          </summary>
          <p>{guide}</p>
          <CodeSnippet
            code={code}
            language="js"
            title="Add below the shared setup"
          />
        </details>
      ))
    }
  </div>

  <h2 id="llama-resources" class="llama-step">Tutorials and resources</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <a href="https://llama.app/docs/quickstart">Llama App quickstart</a>:
      download a model and open its chat.
    </li>
    <li>
      <a href="https://llama.app/docs/api">Llama App API reference</a>: <code
        >GET /v1/models</code
      >, <code>POST /v1/chat/completions</code>, JSON output, and tool calls.
    </li>
    <li>
      <a
        href="https://qwen.readthedocs.io/en/v2.5/getting_started/concepts.html"
        >Qwen’s key concepts</a
      >: how Qwen names its versions and model sizes.
    </li>
  </ul>
</WeekPost>

